m","change":"Bristol-Johnson City's nights have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed.","rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 44\u00b0F was the 11th coolest November 13 high (tied) of 90 since 1937, the coolest since 2022.","t_yest":"November 13: cooler than 88% of years","p_yest":"The high of <strong>44\u00b0F</strong> was the 11th coolest November 13 high (tied) of 90 since 1937, the coolest since 2022. The normal high for the date is 59\u00b0F, and the record is 78\u00b0F (1945). The low of <strong>38\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Record highs and lows, by the decade that set them","p_rec":"If Bristol-Johnson City's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Bristol-Johnson City has set <strong>76 standing record lows</strong>, fewer than the 110 that chance alone would give, and <strong>156 record highs</strong>, more than the 110 that chance alone would give. The 2010s hold the most record highs of any decade (76). Bristol-Johnson City's all-time high is 103\u00b0F, on June 30, 2012.","t_chg":"Bristol-Johnson City's nights have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed.","p_chg":"Across 87 complete years: Bristol-Johnson City's average low shows no clear change: +0.08\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.02 to +0.18) that includes zero. It's close, but the range still includes zero. Bristol-Johnson City's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.18\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.06 to +0.30), about +1.6\u00b0F since 1938. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.39\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.08 to +0.69) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.74\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.38 to +1.09). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 2 of 12 months. Highs rose in 3 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Bristol-Johnson City's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.45 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 44.0 inches a year. The wettest year was 2003 (65.64 in); the driest, 2007 (22.39 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 22 daily record highs, 5 daily record lows"}}